{-
   `alias` - a second name for a type, and deliberately not a type of its own.

   # resolver: ownership

   The whole of what a transparent alias costs is a name table: `resolveType` answers with what it
   names, so nothing below it ever sees one. There is no conversion rule, no coherence question and
   no entry in the ownership classification - which is exactly what a `qualified` alias would need
   all three of, and is why that is a different declaration rather than a flag on this one.

   The targets are read on demand, so an alias may name one written below it and the file has no
   declaration order to keep. `Reject.Alias.yana` beside this is the cycle that makes a walk of them.
-}

alias Count = Whole
alias Whole = I32

-- A head of its own, applied by substituting rather than by interning a record: `Twin(I32)` *is*
-- `Two(I32, I32)`, and the dump below shows one type and not two.
alias Twin(a) = Two(a, a)
alias Pair = Twin(Count)

data Two(a, b) {left: a, right: b}

fn sum(p: Pair) -> Count = p.left + p.right
fn first(t: Twin(I32)) -> Whole = t.left

fn main() -> Count:
    let p = Two {left: 20 :: Count, right: 22 :: I32}
    let q = Two {left: 100 :: I32, right: 0 :: I32}
    return sum(p) + first(q)
